---
import Callout from "@/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Playground from "@/components/Playground.astro";
import { guideGroups, guidePage } from "@/data/guide";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import Heading from "@/components/docs/Heading.astro";

const page = guidePage("/guide/custom-materials");

const contract = `void mainImage( out vec4 mainImage, in vec2 fragCoord ) {
  vec2 uv = fragCoord / uResolution;
  mainImage = textTexture(uv);
}`;

const wobble = `import { Blotter, Material, Text, shaders } from "blotter.ts";

const text = new Text("wobble", {
  family: "'Castoro', serif",
  size: 72,
  fill: "#171717",
  paddingLeft: 40,
  paddingRight: 40,
  paddingTop: 30,
  paddingBottom: 30,
});

const material = new Material({
  mainImage: \`
    \${shaders.noise3d}

    void mainImage( out vec4 mainImage, in vec2 fragCoord ) {
      vec2 uv = fragCoord / uResolution;
      uv.y += snoise(vec3(uv * 4.0, uGlobalTime)) * uAmount;
      mainImage = textTexture(uv);
    }
  \`,
  uniforms: {
    uAmount: { type: "1f", value: 0.05 },
  },
});

const blotter = new Blotter(material, { texts: text });
blotter.forText(text)?.appendTo(output);`;

const uniforms = `const material = new Material({
  mainImage,
  uniforms: {
    uAmount: { type: "1f", value: 0.05 },        // float
    uCenter: { type: "2f", value: [0.5, 0.5] },  // vec2
    uTint:   { type: "3f", value: [1, 0.9, 0.8] }, // vec3
    uEdge:   { type: "4f", value: [0, 0, 0, 1] }, // vec4
  },
});

material.uniforms.uAmount.value = 0.1; // live, like any other uniform`;

const shaderMaterial = `import { ShaderMaterial } from "blotter.ts";

const material = new ShaderMaterial(mainImage, {
  uniforms: { uAmount: { type: "1f", value: 0.05 } },
});`;

const subclass = `import { Material, shaders } from "blotter.ts";

const mainImage = /* glsl */ \`
  \${shaders.noise3d}

  void mainImage( out vec4 mainImage, in vec2 fragCoord ) {
    vec2 uv = fragCoord / uResolution;
    uv.y += snoise(vec3(uv * 4.0, uGlobalTime)) * uAmount;
    mainImage = textTexture(uv);
  }
\`;

export class WobbleMaterial extends Material {
  constructor() {
    super({
      mainImage,
      uniforms: { uAmount: { type: "1f", value: 0.05 } },
    });
  }
}`;

const snippets: [string, string][] = [
  ["noise, noise2d, noise3d, noise4d", "Simplex noise in one to four dimensions (snoise)."],
  ["random", "A hash-based random for a vec2 seed."],
  ["easing", "The Penner easing curves as GLSL functions."],
  ["blending", "Photoshop-style blend modes: multiply, screen, overlay and friends."],
  ["lineMath", "Distance and projection helpers for line segments."],
  ["map", "Remap a value from one range to another."],
  ["gamma", "sRGB to linear and back."],
  ["blinnPhongSpecular", "A specular term for lighting tricks."],
  ["pi, inf", "Constants."],
];
---

<Docs
  title="Custom materials · blotter.ts"
  description={page.description}
  current="guide"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading={page.title}
  lead={page.description}
  groups={guideGroups()}
  sections={page.sections}
>
  <Heading id="why-write-a-shader">Why write a shader</Heading>
  <p>
    The bundled materials cover a lot of ground, but the moment you want a specific look you
    are one function away from having it. A material is a fragment shader in the Shadertoy
    mould: a <code>mainImage</code> function that is called once per pixel and returns a
    colour. Blotter supplies the text as a texture, the uniforms you declare, and a handful of
    built-ins; you supply the pixels.
  </p>
  <p>
    If shaders are new to you, spend ten minutes on <a href="https://shadertoy.com">Shadertoy</a>
    first. Everything there transfers, with one substitution described below.
  </p>

  <Heading id="the-contract">The contract</Heading>
  <p>
    Your <code>mainImage</code> receives the pixel’s coordinate and writes its colour. Sample the
    text with <code>textTexture(uv)</code>, not <code>texture2D</code>: it maps the canvas UV into
    the right region of the shared atlas, and returns transparent black for anything outside
    this text’s bounds. This is the identity material, and the default when you construct a
    <code>Material</code> without one.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={contract} lang="glsl" />
  <p>Every material also receives these uniforms, kept up to date by the renderer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>uResolution</code>, the canvas size in pixels, so <code>fragCoord / uResolution</code> is UV.</li>
    <li><code>uGlobalTime</code>, seconds since the loop started, and <code>uTimeDelta</code>, seconds since the last frame.</li>
    <li><code>uPixelRatio</code>, the device pixel ratio the atlas was drawn at.</li>
    <li><code>uBlendColor</code>, a vec4 some materials composite against. It defaults to white.</li>
  </ul>

  <Heading id="first-material">A first material</Heading>
  <p>
    Start from the identity material and bend the UV before sampling. Here a 3D simplex noise,
    interpolated from the <code>shaders</code> namespace, pushes each row up and down over time.
    The amount is a uniform so it can be tuned while it runs.
  </p>
  <Playground id="wobble" code={wobble} />

  <Heading id="declaring-uniforms">Declaring uniforms</Heading>
  <p>
    Uniforms are declared by name with a type and an initial value. Four types are supported,
    matching the GLSL vector sizes, and the value shape follows the type. Blotter validates the
    pair, declares the GLSL for you, and exposes each one as a live
    <code>UniformInterface</code> on <code>material.uniforms</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={uniforms} lang="ts" />

  <Heading id="shader-material">ShaderMaterial</Heading>
  <p>
    For a one-off effect there is a shorthand. <code>ShaderMaterial</code> takes the
    <code>mainImage</code> string first and the rest of the options second, and is otherwise a
    <code>Material</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={shaderMaterial} lang="ts" />

  <Heading id="subclassing">Subclassing</Heading>
  <p>
    A material you will reuse deserves a name. Subclass <code>Material</code> and pass the shader
    and uniforms up through <code>super</code>; this is exactly how the five bundled materials
    are written, so <code>blotter.ts/materials</code> is a good place to read for patterns.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={subclass} lang="ts" />

  <Heading id="snippets">Shader snippets</Heading>
  <p>
    The <code>shaders</code> export is a namespace of GLSL strings, ready to interpolate at the
    top of a <code>mainImage</code>. They are the helpers the bundled materials use.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Snippet</th><th scope="col">Provides</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        snippets.map(([name, what]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{name}</code></td>
            <td>{what}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <Heading id="gotchas">Gotchas</Heading>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Out of bounds is transparent.</strong> <code>textTexture</code> returns
      <code>vec4(0.0)</code> outside the text’s own rectangle. Displace the UV too far and the
      glyphs simply vanish; add padding to the <code>Text</code> to give an effect room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Built-in names win.</strong> Declaring a uniform called <code>uResolution</code>
      or <code>uGlobalTime</code> is allowed but pointless: the renderer overwrites those values
      every frame.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Blending against the wrong colour.</strong> <code>uBlendColor</code> defaults to
      white. A material that composites through it, such as <code>ChannelSplitMaterial</code>, will
      show pale fringes on a dark page until you set it to your background colour.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Callout title="Debugging a shader">
    <p>
      A compile error surfaces as a rejected <code>blotter.ready</code> with the GLSL log in the
      message. Start from the identity material, add one line at a time, and keep
      <code>uAmount</code> small while you look.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Docs>
